This is because the `DirectoryInfo` has already cached that the directory does not exist when the object was constructed. As you've already noted, calling `Refresh` after `Create` will fix the issue:

```csharp
DirectoryInfo tempDirectoryInfo = new DirectoryInfo("D:\\testdir");

Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
tempDirectoryInfo.Create();
tempDirectoryInfo.Refresh();
Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
tempDirectoryInfo.MoveTo("D:\\testdir2");
```

This no longer reproduces in .NET 5 for me, it appears to have been fixed with https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/34229.

@vcsjones I think the underlying issue is still present even in .net5.

This code works on .net4.7.2 but fails on both core3.1 and net5

```csharp
DirectoryInfo tempDirectoryInfo = new DirectoryInfo("t:\\testdir");
Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
Directory.CreateDirectory("T:\\testdir");
Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
tempDirectoryInfo.MoveTo("t:\\testdir2");
```

I think what is wrong here is the check that executes when `Exists` is false in the `MoveTo` method.
In the check line [here](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/d058930b2bf416635710b8b4022839a1ee1b8e6e/src/libraries/System.IO.FileSystem/src/System/IO/DirectoryInfo.cs#L220)

It reads `!FileSystem.FileExists(FullPath)` when instead it should check `!FileSystem.DirectoryExists(FullPath)`.

@ChristophHornung I am unable to reproduce this issue with .NET 6.0, .NET 5.0 or with .NET Core 3.1.

I wonder if there's something different about your machine that is preventing you from creating the folder.

I ran the below code, in which I made sure to create the `DirectoryInfo` instance after ensuring the folders did not exist, then I created the folder using `Directory.CreateDirectory`, and then I moved it with `DirectoryInfo.MoveTo`. All 3 tests finished successfully:

