@carlossanlop That is because the `MoveTo` in your example happens at a point in time where the underlying information is not yet initialized, and thus an implicit `Refresh` is called. Why didn't you use the example code I provided?

Try this instead to see how checking `Exist` before creating the directory reproduces the issue:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

namespace N
{
	public class C
	{
		public static void Main()
		{
			string root = @"D:\";
			string originalPath = Path.Join(root, "52293_original");
			string movedPath = Path.Join(root, "52293_moved");

			if (Directory.Exists(originalPath))
			{
				Console.WriteLine("Original existed. Deleting.");
				Directory.Delete(originalPath, recursive: true);
			}

			if (Directory.Exists(movedPath))
			{
				Console.WriteLine("Moved existed. Deleting.");
				Directory.Delete(movedPath, recursive: true);
			}

			var info = new DirectoryInfo(originalPath);

			Console.WriteLine($"Creating original: {originalPath}");
			Console.WriteLine("OriginalExists:" + info.Exists);
			Directory.CreateDirectory(originalPath);

			Console.WriteLine("Attempting to move.");
			info.MoveTo(movedPath);

			Console.WriteLine($"Successfully moved: {movedPath}");
		}
	}
}
```

@carlossanlop So the expected behavior for this API changed after .net4? (Since the code executes fine there but only fails on later version) This did crop up in a converting one of my projects to .net core 3.1 and I guess others will run into the same problem when ugprading.

It seems to me the additional `DirectoryExists` call would allow the move to go through in cases where the `DirectoryInfo` object does not have the latest information about the directory, I don't see any harm in that behavior. The tests break because after the change a `DirectoryNotFoundException` is thrown instead of the expected `IOException`. I would argue that is the more correction exception to throw here since if `Exist` is false the same kind of exception is thrown.

When this really is the expected behavior, shouldn't at least a remark also be added to the documentation of the `MoveTo` method to mention than an exception is thrown when the `DirectoryInfo` is out of sync though the directory exists in the file system? (Additionally the documentation currently does not even mention that the `DirectoryNotFoundException` is thrown when the source directory does not exists)

