Exists does not know if the directory 'really' exists on the file system. (See e.g. my test via a `Directory.CreateDirectory` in #52293) 

I think the behavior should be the same way it was in .net4.x. That is if the `Exists` flag is false we should still check with the file system directly to see if the directory exists or not. (If the exists flag is incorrectly `true` it will fail on the move call to the file system later).

In the end the code that worked in the .net4.x should still work. E.g.:

```csharp
DirectoryInfo tempDirectoryInfo = new DirectoryInfo("t:\\testdir");
Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
Directory.CreateDirectory("T:\\testdir");
Debug.WriteLine(tempDirectoryInfo.Exists);
tempDirectoryInfo.MoveTo("t:\\testdir2");
```

Good point! You are right, there seems to be a similar issue in `FileInfo.MoveTo`

This code does not throw an exception on .net4 but does on .net5:

```csharp
// Make sure the file does not exist yet
FileInfo tempFileInfo = new FileInfo("t:\\test.txt");
Debug.WriteLine(tempFileInfo.Exists);
File.Create("T:\\test.txt").Dispose();
Debug.WriteLine(tempFileInfo.Exists);
tempFileInfo.MoveTo("t:\\test2.txt");
```

I think the exists check [here](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/0390d2dcb8e620ad297029596553b44da1a4b6b5/src/libraries/System.IO.FileSystem/src/System/IO/FileInfo.cs#L156) should do the same kind of check via `FileSystem.FileExists`
